    text: `Prejudice refers to a negative attitude toward members of a social group; discrimination refers to differential behavior directed at group members based on group membership. While prejudice involves affective (emotional) evaluation, stereotypes are the cognitive component — beliefs (accurate or inaccurate) about characteristics shared by group members. Stereotypes can be activated automatically, without conscious intention, and can influence behavior even when the individual consciously rejects the stereotype.

The Implicit Association Test (IAT), developed by Greenwald and colleagues, measures implicit attitudes by assessing reaction times when category-concept pairings are congruent versus incongruent. Faster responses when Black faces are paired with negative words than when paired with positive words indicate an implicit racial bias favoring White individuals, independent of what the respondent reports believing consciously. Implicit biases predict behavior in certain contexts (hiring decisions, medical treatment recommendations) but the relationship between implicit bias and behavior is moderated by: context (situations that prompt deliberation reduce implicit-behavior effects), motivation to control prejudice, and individual differences.

Stereotype threat, identified by Claude Steele, occurs when awareness of a negative stereotype about one's group impairs performance on a task to which the stereotype is relevant. Stereotype threat effects have been demonstrated across diverse groups (women in mathematics, Black students in academic achievement tests, elderly individuals in memory tests) and are mediated by increased anxiety, cognitive load, and reduced working memory capacity. Interventions that reduce stereotype threat include: affirming personal values (self-affirmation theory), providing role models who have succeeded despite stereotyping, and framing tasks as non-diagnostic of ability.`,
