    title: 'Socialization, Culture, and Norms',
    text: `Socialization is the lifelong process through which individuals learn and internalize the norms, values, beliefs, and behaviors appropriate to their culture. Primary socialization occurs in childhood through family (the most influential early agent) and establishes foundational values and personality traits. Secondary socialization occurs throughout life through institutions such as schools, peer groups, and media, introducing individuals to role-specific norms and behaviors.

Social norms are shared expectations about behavior within a group. Norms may be descriptive (what most people do) or injunctive (what most people approve or disapprove of). Compliance with norms is maintained through social sanctions — positive (praise, reward) or negative (ostracism, punishment). Social norms are internalized when individuals come to genuinely endorse them; they are merely complied with when individuals conform to avoid sanction without genuine endorsement.

Culture consists of shared beliefs, values, norms, practices, symbols, and material artifacts that characterize a group. Cross-cultural research distinguishes between individualistic cultures (emphasizing personal goals, autonomy, and individual identity — common in Western nations) and collectivist cultures (emphasizing group goals, interdependence, and social roles — common in East Asian, Latin American, and African nations). These orientations influence a wide range of psychological phenomena, including self-concept, attribution styles, emotional expression, and mental health patterns.

Medicalization refers to the process by which non-medical conditions come to be defined and treated as medical problems. The example of homosexuality (removed from the DSM in 1973) illustrates how social and political forces shape what is considered a psychiatric disorder. Critics argue that medicalization can pathologize normal variation and expand pharmaceutical market reach; proponents note that it can reduce stigma, provide access to care, and promote treatment-seeking.`,
