    title: 'Social Class, Inequality, and Social Mobility',
    text: `Social stratification refers to the hierarchical arrangement of social groups based on dimensions of inequality, including socioeconomic status (SES), which is typically measured as a composite of income, education, and occupational prestige. Sociological theories of stratification differ in their explanations of inequality's origins and persistence.

Functionalist theory (Davis and Moore) argues that stratification is universal and functional: higher-status positions require greater talent and training, and differential rewards are necessary to motivate qualified individuals to fill them. Conflict theory (Marx, Weber) views stratification as the result of power differentials — dominant groups use economic and cultural resources to maintain their advantage. Pierre Bourdieu's theory of capital extends this analysis: social inequality is perpetuated through not just economic capital (money, property) but also cultural capital (education, tastes, credentials) and social capital (social networks and relationships). Symbolic capital refers to the prestige and recognition derived from the accumulation of other capitals.

Social mobility refers to movement between social strata. Vertical mobility is movement up (upward) or down (downward) the social hierarchy. Horizontal mobility is movement between positions of similar status. Intergenerational mobility (comparing children's SES to their parents') is a key measure of social fluidity. Research consistently shows that the United States has lower intergenerational mobility than many European nations, despite its cultural narrative of meritocracy.

The Thomas theorem holds that "if people define situations as real, they are real in their consequences." This has implications for how social class affects behavior: individuals who perceive themselves as low-status may limit their aspirations and opportunities, regardless of objective social position — a self-fulfilling dynamic consistent with labeling theory.`,
