    passageNumber: 9,
    title: 'Behavior Genetics and the Nature-Nurture Interaction',
    text: `Behavior genetics investigates the genetic and environmental contributions to individual differences in psychological traits. Twin studies are the primary methodology: monozygotic (MZ) twins share 100% of their genetic material; dizygotic (DZ) twins share on average 50%. If a trait has a heritable component, MZ twins will show greater concordance (similarity) than DZ twins. Heritability (h²) is a population statistic estimating the proportion of phenotypic variance in a population that is attributable to genetic variance. Importantly, heritability does not indicate how much of a trait is "caused" by genes in an absolute sense — it estimates the relative contribution of genetic variance given a particular environment.

Environment is divided into two components: shared environment (aspects of environment common to siblings raised together — parenting style, socioeconomic status, neighborhood) and nonshared environment (experiences unique to each individual — different peer groups, sibling-specific interactions, idiosyncratic life events). Behavioral genetic research consistently finds that for most adult personality traits, heritability is substantial (~40-60%), shared environment effects are minimal, and nonshared environment (plus measurement error) accounts for most remaining variance.

Gene-environment interaction (GxE) refers to the finding that genetic factors moderate the effect of environmental exposures (or vice versa). The landmark Caspi et al. (2003) study demonstrated that individuals with the short allele of the serotonin transporter gene (5-HTTLPR) showed greater depression risk in response to stressful life events than those with the long allele — but only in stressful environments. In a low-stress environment, genotype did not predict depression risk.

Gene-environment correlation (rGE) refers to the non-random distribution of genotypes across environments. Passive rGE: parents provide both genes and environment (reading genes from parents → also a book-rich home). Evocative rGE: the child's genetic characteristics evoke responses from others (an irritable child elicits harsh parenting). Active rGE: individuals select environments that match their genetic predispositions (extroverts seek social environments).`,
